## The Arnolfini Portrait: who else is in the room?
sources:
    - id: ng
      title: The Arnolfini Portrait (NG186)
      authors: The National Gallery, London
      year: 2024
      finding: The sitters, the room's luxuries, two men in the mirror 'coming in through a door behind us', the signature 'Johannes de Eyck fuit hic. 1434', no longer read as a marriage, 'his thumbprint by the shadow of the dog's leg', the Spanish royal collection
      url: https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/jan-van-eyck-the-arnolfini-portrait
    - id: panofsky
      title: Jan van Eyck's 'Arnolfini' Portrait
      authors: Erwin Panofsky
      year: 1934
      finding: 'Burlington Magazine: the picture as a record of a marriage, with the painter''s signature as a witness''s'
    - id: wiki
      title: Arnolfini Portrait
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: The dog looks out at the viewer; the red figure presumably the artist; in the Alcázar in Madrid by 1599, very likely known to Velázquez
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnolfini_Portrait
    - id: turban
      title: Portrait of a Man (Self Portrait?)
      authors: Jan van Eyck, National Gallery, London
      year: 1433
      finding: Frame inscribed 'Johes de Eyck me fecit' (Jan van Eyck made me), 21 October 1433; a man in a red headdress

    card title "The Arnolfini Portrait"

    image art cites ["ng"] credit "Jan van Eyck, 1434. National Gallery, London. Public domain." x 271 y 342 width 462 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Van_Eyck_-_Arnolfini_Portrait.jpg/1920px-Van_Eyck_-_Arnolfini_Portrait.jpg look screen regions.backs [0.478,0.279,0.058,0.05] regions.chandelier [0.37,0,0.286,0.198] regions.dog [0.365,0.823,0.208,0.16] regions.dog_face [0.486,0.842,0.088,0.061] regions.dog_legs [0.37,0.925,0.21,0.065] regions.foreground [0,0.86,1,0.14] regions.joined_hands [0.505,0.396,0.11,0.044] regions.man [0.068,0.11,0.443,0.796] regions.mirror [0.426,0.241,0.158,0.116] regions.mirror_glass [0.457,0.264,0.094,0.069] regions.oranges [0.071,0.522,0.046,0.023] regions.raised_hand [0.231,0.293,0.042,0.08] regions.room [0.25,0.15,0.5,0.45] regions.signature [0.418,0.193,0.14,0.042] regions.visitors [0.494,0.285,0.034,0.036] regions.woman [0.536,0.171,0.453,0.812]

Jan van Eyck painted this couple in Bruges in 1434, and our question is: who else is in the room?
    camera on ["art:room"] zoom 1.5 duration 6

    camera reset true

At first sight there are two people: an Italian merchant, probably Giovanni Arnolfini, and his wife.
    highlight art:man art:woman

Everything around them says money, from the brass chandelier to the oranges, an expensive import.
    highlight art:chandelier art:oranges

He raises one hand and takes hers with the other, as if making a promise.
    highlight art:raised_hand art:joined_hands

And the little dog looks straight out of the picture, at us.
    highlight art:dog_face effect detail

    ask "Where are they? Tap the place." spot mirror on art
        intro: Two people and a dog, then. But van Eyck put more people in this room.
        right: Yes: the little round mirror on the back wall.
        wrong: Look at the back wall, between the two of them.

On the back wall hangs a convex mirror, bulging outward, so it gathers the whole room into one small circle.
    highlight art:mirror_glass effect detail

In it, we see the couple again, from behind.
    highlight art:backs effect detail

And facing them, in the doorway, two more people: one in blue, one in red.
    highlight art:visitors effect detail

That door is behind us.
    highlight art:visitors effect detail

They stand on our side of the room, just where we stand to look.
    highlight art:foreground strength strong

Above the mirror, in looping letters, the painter wrote on the wall in Latin: Jan van Eyck was here, 1434.
    highlight art:signature effect detail

Now look back into the glass.
    highlight art:visitors effect detail

    quiz "Who might the figure in red be?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 770 buttons_at.y 560 buttons_layout column
        right: Many think so: the man who signed the wall stands in the doorway.
        wrong: Read the words painted just above the mirror.
        * A servant
        + The painter
        * A priest

    image turban cites ["turban"] credit "Van Eyck, 1433. National Gallery, London." x 770 y 360 width 340 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Jan_van_Eyck_-_Portrait_of_a_Man_%28Self_Portrait%3F%29_1433_Frame.jpg/960px-Jan_van_Eyck_-_Portrait_of_a_Man_%28Self_Portrait%3F%29_1433_Frame.jpg look screen regions.inscription [0,0.935,1,0.065] regions.turban [0.16,0.11,0.62,0.37] reveal develop

A portrait thought to be van Eyck himself, painted a year earlier, shows a man in a red headdress too.
    highlight turban:turban

On its frame he wrote: Jan van Eyck  (@highlight turban:inscription art:signature@)made me. Here he writes that he was present.

    erase turban

That is how a witness signs, and in 1934 t (@highlight art:signature art:mirror_glass effect detail@)he art historian Erwin Panofsky read the whole picture as a wedding, with van Eyck as its witness.

Many now doubt it was a wedding at all, but the witness in the glass is still there.
    highlight art:visitors effect detail

But he was here in another way too: beside the dog's leg, his thumbprint is pressed into the paint.
    highlight art:dog_legs effect detail

So the mirror shows what the room itself can't: (@highlight art:mirror_glass art:foreground@) the people standing on our side of it.

Van Eyck signs himself into that doorway, and leaves room there for us, as witnesses too.
    highlight art:mirror_glass effect detail

    quiz "A photographer wants one shot of a bride that shows the guests watching, without turning the camera. What would van Eyck do?"
        right: A mirror behind her: it shows the room on the camera's side, guests, photographer and all.
        wrong: The camera can't turn round, so something in the room has to look back.
        * Shoot from far back
        + Hang a mirror behind the bride
        * Ask the bride to look at the guests
        * Write the guests' names on the wall

Two centuries later this panel hung in the royal palace in Madrid, where Velázquez very likely came to know it.
    highlight art:mirror lift true

